nudge — put the pure part in src/lib/nudge.ts so it is testable without a DOM:
```ts
export function nudge(
	a: number, b: number, which: 'a' | 'b', d: number, last: number
): [number, number] {
	if (which === 'a') {
		const n = Math.min(Math.max(0, a + d), b);
		return [n, b];
	}
	const n = Math.max(Math.min(last, b + d), a);
	return [a, n];
}
```
The clamps are the whole point: `a` can never pass `b`, `b` can never pass `a`, and a === b is legal and means a single-frame still. Do not "fix" that by forcing a minimum length.

Decode discipline — key repeat can fire faster than a decode completes, so latest-wins:
```ts
let token = 0;
async function refresh() {
	const mine = ++token;
	const f = focus === 'a' ? a : b;
	const smp = await frame_at(ui.src_sel, f, r.f);
	if (mine !== token) return void smp?.close?.();
	if (smp && 'toVideoFrame' in smp) {
		big?.close();
		big = await createImageBitmap(smp.toVideoFrame());
		smp.close();
	}
}
```
The filmstrip runs on the same token but debounced 120ms (clearTimeout/setTimeout), because 7 decodes per keypress during a held arrow key would stall the decoder queue. It uses sinks.get(id)!.samplesAtTimestamps(offsets.map(o => (f + o + 0.5) / r.f)) — one sequential pass, far cheaper than 7 getSample seeks. Close every VideoSample and every replaced ImageBitmap; leaking VideoFrames stalls the decoder after a few hundred frames and is the single most likely bug in this step.

Pictures (r.k === 'p'): skip all decoding, show the bitmap, and let ←/→ change b only, as a DURATION in project frames (a stays 0). "add clip" then produces a still of that length. Because pictures were given f = p.f at import, item_len needs no special case.

